-- title:
--   Degree n over k(y) of the fraction field of a rank-n order
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ and $E$ be fields with $E$ a $k$-algebra, let $n$ be a natural number, let $y \in E$, and let $s : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to E$ be a family of $n$ elements of $E$. Assume three conditions, all phrased with coefficients from the polynomial ring $k[X]$ evaluated at $y$: (i) for all $i,j$ there are polynomials $c_l \in k[X]$ with $s_i s_j = \sum_l c_l(y)\, s_l$, so the $k[y]$-span $M$ of the $s_i$ is closed under multiplication; (ii) for every family $c : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to k[X]$, the relation $\sum_i c_i(y)\, s_i = 0$ forces $c = 0$, i.e. the $s_i$ are linearly independent over $k[y]$ in the strong sense that the coefficient polynomials themselves vanish; (iii) every $z \in E$ can be written as a quotient of two elements of $M$, namely there are families $cu, cv : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to k[X]$ with $v := \sum_i cv_i(y)\, s_i \neq 0$ and $z\,v = \sum_i cu_i(y)\, s_i$. The conclusion is the conjunction: $E$ is finite-dimensional as a module over the intermediate field $k(y) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin k {y}` of $E$, and its rank over that intermediate field equals $n$.
--
--   This is the elementary statement that the field of fractions of an order which is free of rank $n$ over $k[y]$ has degree $n$ over the subfield generated by $y$; it exhibits $s_1,\dots,s_n$ as a basis of $E$ over $k(y)$. It is used in computing the degree of a generic fibre, in the construction of good constant reductions feeding [`AlgebraicCurve.exists_constantReduction_isGood_of_wittVector_normalFormOrder`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.exists_constantReduction_isGood_of_wittVector_normalFormOrder).
